  # Why: The central authorisation rule — no active relationship, no delegated voice access (mirrors FEAT-POC-003 / FEAT-POC-004)
  @negative @smoke
  Scenario: An attorney with no relationship to a donor cannot voice-query their flags
    Given I am an attorney with no relationship to a particular donor
    When I ask the platform to talk me through that donor's flags by voice
    Then I should be refused because I am not linked to that donor
    And no narration should be produced

  # Why: Only ACTIVE relationships confer access — pending, dormant and revoked all deny
  @negative
  Scenario Outline: Only an active relationship grants delegated voice access
    Given I am an attorney whose relationship with the donor is <status>
    When I ask the platform to talk me through that donor's flags by voice
    Then I should be refused because I am not actively linked to that donor

    Examples:
      | status  |
      | pending |
      | dormant |
      | revoked |

  # Why: Donor-to-donor isolation — flags are personal to the donor
  @negative
  Scenario: A donor cannot voice-query another donor's flags
    Given another donor has flagged transactions
    When I ask the platform to talk me through that other donor's flags by voice
    Then I should be refused

  # Why: All voice surfaces require a signed-in user
  @negative
  Scenario: Voice review is not available without signing in
    Given I am not signed in
    When I ask the platform to talk me through a flag by voice
    Then I should be asked to sign in
    And no narration should be produced

  # Why: STT must not fabricate meaning — an unintelligible question is reported, not guessed at
  @negative
  Scenario: A question the platform cannot make sense of is reported rather than guessed
    Given a recording the platform cannot transcribe into a meaningful question
    When I submit it as a voice query
    Then I should be told the question could not be understood
    And no narration should be fabricated

  # Why: An empty or silent recording has nothing to transcribe
  @negative @boundary
  Scenario: An empty or silent recording is refused
    Given a recording that contains no speech
    When I submit it as a voice query
    Then it should be refused as containing nothing to transcribe
    And no narration should be produced

  # Why: Voice is read-only narration — it must never become a command channel that changes state (delegation is read-only)
  @negative
  Scenario: A spoken command attempting to change something is refused
    Given I am an attorney with an active relationship to the donor
    When I ask by voice to approve or change one of the donor's transactions
    Then I should be refused because voice can only explain, not change anything
    And nothing about the transaction should be altered

