  # Why: THE privacy invariant of ADR-POC-015 Decision 5 — inbound query audio is transcribed then discarded, never at rest
  # [ASSUMPTION: confidence=high] ASSUM-009 — inbound query audio is discarded after transcription and never persisted
  @edge-case @negative @smoke
  Scenario: Inbound query audio is discarded after transcription and never persisted
    Given I ask a question by voice about a flag
    When the platform has transcribed my question
    Then the recording of my question should be discarded
    And the recording should never be persisted anywhere

  # Why: Narration audio may be cached for replay but is never durable — no audio in the database (ADR-POC-015 Decision 5)
  # [ASSUMPTION: confidence=high] ASSUM-010 — no audio is ever stored in the database
  @edge-case
  Scenario: Narration audio is only ever held ephemerally and never stored in the database
    Given a narration has been produced for a flag and later replayed from cache
    Then the narration audio should only ever be held ephemerally for replay
    And no narration audio should ever be stored in the database

  # Why: The whole FCA/FAPI pitch — when local voice is down there is NO cloud failover; audio never leaves our infrastructure (ADR-POC-015 Decision 2 / DECISION-DF-001)
  @edge-case @negative @smoke
  Scenario: When local voice is unavailable audio is never sent to a third party
    Given the local voice service is unavailable
    When I try to ask a question by voice
    Then my recording should not be sent to any third-party service
    And the feature should degrade to the text view instead

  # Why: The transcript is untrusted text — a spoken instruction must not steer the rule reasoning (mirrors FEAT-POC-004 §E injection)
  @edge-case @negative
  Scenario: A spoken instruction attempting to manipulate the reasoning has no effect
    Given I speak a question that also instructs the platform to ignore the donor's rules
    When the platform transcribes and answers my question
    Then the donor's actual rules should still be applied unchanged
    And the narration should not suppress or fabricate any flag

  # Why: The audit trail is the FCA narrative — voice events must be tamper-evident (append-only)
  @edge-case @negative
  Scenario: Voice audit events cannot be altered or removed
    Given voice query and narration events have been recorded in the audit log
    When any attempt is made through the application to alter or delete those records
    Then the records should remain exactly as first written

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # GROUP F: Expansion — Concurrency
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  # Why: Donor and attorney can review the same flag simultaneously — narration must be correct and isolated per requester
  @edge-case
  Scenario: A donor and their attorney narrating the same flag at once each receive correct narration
    Given the donor and an attorney with an active relationship both open the same flagged transaction
    When they each ask to be talked through that flag at the same time
    Then each of them should receive the narration explaining that flag
    And neither one's narration should be affected by the other's request

  # Why: A narration must pin to one rules version — never half from before and half from after a correction in flight
  @edge-case
  Scenario: A narration requested while the rules are changing reflects one consistent set of rules
    Given a correction to the donor's rules is being applied
    When I ask to be talked through a flag at the same time
    Then the narration should reflect a single consistent set of rules
    And it should not mix the rules from before and after the correction

